# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

criminology | definition | Criminology is the study of crime, offenders and the systems that respond to them, and the discipline is defined by its causes as much as by its effects. Its largest methodological problem is that the data comes from police records, which count what was reported and detected rather than what happened, so most crimes are measured by a system built to record a different number. The field has consequently shifted towards self-report surveys and victim studies, which capture more but have their own problems. | criminology, crime, offender, police, data, report | what is criminology, is criminology easy, what does a criminologist do | criminology | kriminolohiya
rehabilitation | definition | Rehabilitation is the goal of reducing a person's likelihood of reoffending, and it is the least popular of the three purposes of punishment, which are retribution, deterrence and reform. The evidence broadly supports it for reducing reoffending, and the mechanism is thought to be a changed life rather than a changed attitude, since skills and housing move the number more reliably than instruction. What works is rarely dramatic: stable accommodation, work, and treating addiction as a health problem rather than a character one. | rehabilitation, reoffend, punishment, deterrence, reform, prison | what is rehabilitation, does prison reduce crime, what is the point of prison | rehabilitation | rehabilitasyon
deterrence | definition | Deterrence is the attempt to prevent crime by making the consequences known and severe enough to outweigh the gain, and the evidence for it is weak. People rarely calculate the expected penalty before deciding, and the ones most likely to commit an offence are the least likely to be deterred by it. General deterrence, aimed at everyone, has almost no measurable effect; specific deterrence, aimed at somebody who has already offended, has more, mainly because prison is genuinely unpleasant. | deterrence, prevent, crime, penalty, severe, prison | what is deterrence, does harsher punishment reduce crime, is deterrence effective | deterrence | deterrente
why people commit crime | definition | People commit crime for a small set of reasons that researchers keep failing to find a single one of, and the strongest predictors are practical rather than moral. Poverty alone explains little, since most people in poverty do not offend. The better-supported factors are a criminal record already, being male and young, substance use, and the local opportunity, which is why a street with no supervision and plenty of targets sees more of it. Almost all of these are chances rather than choices, which is the uncomfortable part of the finding. | why people commit crime, cause, poverty, record, opportunity, age | why do people commit crime, is it because of poverty, what causes crime | why people commit crime | bakit nag-aangal
